{"causes": ["The gradual decentralization of power during the late Middle Kingdom led to the fragmented nature of the 13th Dynasty.", "The preceding reigns of his brothers, Neferhotep I and Sihathor, established a familial claim to the throne and a precedent for building projects.", "The need for a strong central authority to manage internal divisions and maintain control over trade routes compelled a more assertive pharaonic role."], "effects": ["Sobekhotep IV's reign temporarily stabilized the fragmented 13th Dynasty and reasserted pharaonic authority through extensive building projects.", "His monumental constructions, particularly at Karnak, contributed significantly to the religious infrastructure of Egypt and reinforced the cult of Amun.", "The continued Egyptian presence in Nubia during his rule facilitated cultural exchange and maintained access to vital resources like gold and timber."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "neferhotep-i", "sourceName": "Neferhotep I", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "sobekhotep-iv", "targetName": "Sobekhotep IV", "context": "Sobekhotep IV succeeded his brother Neferhotep I on the throne of Egypt during the 13th Dynasty around 1729 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "sobekhotep-iv", "sourceName": "Sobekhotep IV", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "temple-of-amun-karnak", "targetName": "Temple of Amun at Karnak", "context": "Sobekhotep IV undertook significant construction and additions to the Temple of Amun at Karnak, enhancing its grandeur."}, {"sourceSlug": "second-intermediate-period", "sourceName": "Second Intermediate Period", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "sobekhotep-iv", "targetName": "Sobekhotep IV", "context": "The reign of Sobekhotep IV falls squarely within the turbulent Second Intermediate Period of ancient Egyptian history (c. 1782–1550 BCE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "sobekhotep-iv", "sourceName": "Sobekhotep IV", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-egypt", "targetName": "Ancient Egypt", "context": "Sobekhotep IV's reign as pharaoh was entirely situated within the geographical and political boundaries of Ancient Egypt."}, {"sourceSlug": "hyksos-invasion", "sourceName": "Hyksos Invasion", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "sobekhotep-iv", "targetName": "Sobekhotep IV", "context": "The growing instability and eventual Hyksos invasion (c. 1650 BCE) created a context of decline that Sobekhotep IV's reign attempted to counter."}], "places": [{"name": "Thebes, Egypt", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Karnak, Egypt", "role": "Major Construction Site"}, {"name": "Abydos, Egypt", "role": "Cult Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Sobekhotep IV", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "sobekhotep-iv"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q373539", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sobekhotep_IV"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Sobekhotep IV represents a significant regional effort to reassert centralized pharaonic power during the fragmented 13th Dynasty, leaving behind substantial monumental evidence of his reign. His rule temporarily stemmed the tide of decline and influenced subsequent rulers' attempts to restore royal authority before the Hyksos invasion.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:23:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30793058330", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Khaneferre Sobekhotep IV was one of the more powerful Egyptian kings of the 13th Dynasty, who reigned at least eight years. His brothers, Neferhotep I and Sihathor, were his predecessors on the throne, the latter having only ruled as coregent for a few months.", "newValue": "Khaneferre Sobekhotep IV, who reigned approximately 1729–1719 BCE, was a pivotal pharaoh of Egypt's fragmented 13th Dynasty during the Second Intermediate Period. Emerging from a period of dynastic instability, his rule marked a temporary resurgence of centralized power and royal authority, challenging the gradual decline of the Middle Kingdom and foreshadowing the eventual rise of the Hyksos. His reign, lasting at least eight years, saw significant building projects and assertive foreign policy, distinguishing him among his often ephemeral predecessors and successors within a period of profound political flux.

Ascending the throne after his brothers, Neferhotep I and the short-lived coregent Sihathor, Sobekhotep IV inherited a realm grappling with internal divisions and the growing independence of regional nomarchs. Despite these challenges, he initiated ambitious construction programs, notably adding to the Temple of Amun at Karnak and erecting monuments at Abydos and Elephantine, underscoring his claim to traditional pharaonic legitimacy. His administration also maintained trade relations, evidenced by his name found on artifacts as far as Byblos, and asserted Egyptian control over Nubia, as suggested by inscriptions and stelae in the region.

Sobekhotep IV's reign represents a brief, but notable, attempt to restore the grandeur of the Middle Kingdom. His efforts to consolidate royal power and engage in public works temporarily halted the accelerating decentralization that characterized the 13th Dynasty, providing a model of pharaonic assertiveness that would be emulated, albeit often unsuccessfully, by later rulers in the period. The impressive scale of his monuments and the geographical reach of his attested influence suggest a more robust state apparatus than previously assumed for this era, offering a valuable counterpoint to narratives of unmitigated decline."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:23:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30793058330", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1700 BCE, Thebes"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1700 BCE, Thebes"}], "newValue": ["The gradual decentralization of power during the late Middle Kingdom led to the fragmented nature of the 13th Dynasty.", "The preceding reigns of his brothers, Neferhotep I and Sihathor, established a familial claim to the throne and a precedent for building projects.", "The need for a strong central authority to manage internal divisions and maintain control over trade routes compelled a more assertive pharaonic role."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-03T07:23:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30793058330", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1719 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1719 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Sobekhotep IV's reign temporarily stabilized the fragmented 13th Dynasty and reasserted pharaonic 